<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Carlist State</h1><p class="st-meta">1872 CE – 1876 CE · capital Estella</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The counter-state of the Carlist pretender Carlos VII in the Basque Country and Navarre during the Third Carlist War, with its capital at Estella.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1872, as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sexenio-democratico">Sexenio Democrático</a> unsettled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, the traditionalist pretender <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-duke-of-madrid">Carlos VII</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-carlist-war">Third Carlist War</a> under the banner of God, Fatherland and King. His forces took control of much of rural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, the Basque provinces and parts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Catalonia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlism">Carlists</a> organised a real administration from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estella-lizarra">Estella</a>: ministries, courts, a postal service with its own stamps, coinage and a university at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onati">Oñati</a>. They restored the Basque and Navarrese fueros and enforced strict <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>. Their armies besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilbao">Bilbao</a> in 1874 but never held a major city for long.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ile-bourbon">Bourbon</a> restoration in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/restoration-spain">Restoration Spain</a>, government armies advanced. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estella-lizarra">Estella</a> fell in February 1876, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-duke-of-madrid">Carlos VII</a> crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The Basque fueros were then largely abolished, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlism">Carlism</a> survived as a political movement.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 1872 CE – 1876 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M30 82 V46 Q50 12 70 46 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 30 V82 M40 58 H60"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Iberia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:96.96%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1872 CE – 1876 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Estella</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Carlos VII</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish, Basque</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Carlist Spain</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1874&amp;focus=carlist-state" aria-label="Carlist State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4640 689 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Carlist State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4960 945L4909 989M4960 945L4909 989"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4909 989h0M4909 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4960 945h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4960 945h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Estella. <a href="/atlas?year=1874&amp;focus=carlist-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
